   {"text": "At B, because the slopes of AB and BC multiply to -1", "feedback": "Correct."},
   {"text": "At C, because C is on the x axis", "feedback": "Sitting on an axis does not create a right angle."}
 ]'::jsonb,
 null),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 36, 'Challenge',
 'To prove a quadrilateral is a parallelogram using slopes, what must be shown?', 2,
 '[
   {"text": "All four sides of the quadrilateral have the same slope", "feedback": "All the same slope would flatten the shape onto one line."},
   {"text": "The two diagonals of the quadrilateral have equal slopes", "feedback": "Equal-slope diagonals would be parallel and could never cross inside the shape."},
   {"text": "Both pairs of opposite sides have equal slopes", "feedback": "Correct."},
   {"text": "Only one pair of opposite sides has equal slopes", "feedback": "One pair only makes a trapezoid. A parallelogram needs both."}
 ]'::jsonb,
 null),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 37, 'Challenge',
 'The shortest distance from a point to a line is measured along which path?', 0,
 '[
   {"text": "The perpendicular from the point to the line", "feedback": "Correct."},
   {"text": "The segment to the nearest endpoint of the line", "feedback": "A line has no endpoints. The shortest route always meets it at 90 degrees."},
   {"text": "A segment parallel to the line", "feedback": "A parallel path never reaches the line at all."},
   {"text": "The segment to the y-intercept of the line", "feedback": "The y-intercept is just one point on the line, rarely the closest one."}
 ]'::jsonb,
 null),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 38, 'Advanced',
 'A line passes through (1, 4) and is perpendicular to the line 2x + 3y = 12. What is the equation of the line?', 1,
 '[{"text": "y = −²⁄₃x + ²⁴⁄₃", "feedback": "This uses the original line''s slope. A perpendicular line needs the negative reciprocal of it instead."}, {"text": "y = ³⁄₂x + ⁵⁄₂", "feedback": "Correct. 2x + 3y = 12 rearranges to y = −²⁄₃x + 4, so the perpendicular slope is ³⁄₂; substituting (1, 4) into y = ³⁄₂x + b gives b = ⁵⁄₂."}, {"text": "y = ²⁄₃x + ¹⁰⁄₃", "feedback": "This keeps the original slope''s size but flips only the sign, which still gives a line parallel-ish in steepness rather than the true negative reciprocal."}, {"text": "y = −³⁄₂x + ¹¹⁄₂", "feedback": "The slope''s size is right, but its sign is not — the original slope is negative, so its negative reciprocal should be positive."}]'::jsonb,
 'perpendicular-negative-reciprocal'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 39, 'Advanced',
 'Quadrilateral ABCD has A(0, 0), B(3, 4), C(7, 1), and D(4, −3). Using both slope and length, what is the most precise name for this shape?', 3,
 '[{"text": "Rhombus (but not a square)", "feedback": "All four sides are equal, but check the slopes of adjacent sides too — they may also be perpendicular."}, {"text": "Rectangle", "feedback": "A rectangle only needs right angles, not equal side lengths — but here all four sides actually come out equal, which is a stronger result."}, {"text": "Parallelogram (but not a rhombus)", "feedback": "All four side lengths actually come out equal here (each is 5), which is more than a general parallelogram guarantees."}, {"text": "Square", "feedback": "Correct. Every side measures √(3²+4²) = 5 (a rhombus), and the slopes of AB (⁴⁄₃) and BC (−³⁄₄) multiply to −1, so adjacent sides are also perpendicular — a rhombus with right angles is a square."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadrilateral-classification-multi-step'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 40, 'Advanced',
 'A line passes through (1, 2) and is parallel to the line through (3, −1) and (7, 5). What is the new line''s equation?', 1,
 '[{"text": "y = ³⁄₂x − 1", "feedback": "Substituting (1, 2) into this equation gives 2 = 1.5 − 1 = 0.5, which is false — recheck the value of b."}, {"text": "y = ³⁄₂x + ½", "feedback": "Correct. The given line has slope (5−(−1))÷(7−3) = 3/2; substituting (1, 2) into y = 3/2 x + b gives 2 = 3/2 + b, so b = 1/2."}, {"text": "y = −²⁄₃x + ⁸⁄₃", "feedback": "This uses the negative reciprocal of 3/2, which belongs to a perpendicular line, not a parallel one."}, {"text": "y = ³⁄₂x + 2", "feedback": "This uses the new point''s y-coordinate directly as the intercept, without solving 2 = 3/2(1) + b for b."}]'::jsonb,
 'parallel-line-through-point-two-point-slope'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 41, 'Advanced',
 'What is the equation of the perpendicular bisector of the segment from (2, 6) to (8, 2)?', 2,
 '[{"text": "y = −²⁄₃x + ¹⁶⁄₃", "feedback": "This keeps the segment''s own slope — a perpendicular bisector needs the negative reciprocal of that slope instead."}, {"text": "y = ³⁄₂x − ⁷⁄₂, through (2, 6)", "feedback": "This equation doesn''t actually pass through the segment''s midpoint — recompute the midpoint of (2,6) and (8,2) first."}, {"text": "y = ³⁄₂x − ⁷⁄₂", "feedback": "Correct. The segment''s midpoint is (5, 4) and its slope is (2−6)÷(8−2) = −2/3, so the perpendicular bisector has slope 3/2 and passes through (5,4): 4 = 3/2(5) + b gives b = −7/2."}, {"text": "y = −²⁄₃x + 4", "feedback": "This uses the segment''s own slope (not its negative reciprocal) and doesn''t pass through the midpoint (5, 4) either."}]'::jsonb,
 'perpendicular-bisector'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 42, 'Advanced',
 'For what value of k are the lines y = 2x + 3 and y = −½x + k perpendicular?', 3,
 '[{"text": "k = 3", "feedback": "This reuses the first line''s own y-intercept — perpendicularity depends only on the slopes, not on matching intercepts."}, {"text": "k = −½", "feedback": "This reuses the second line''s own slope value as if it needed to relate to k some other way."}, {"text": "k = 2", "feedback": "This reuses the first line''s slope — but k here is the second line''s y-intercept, unrelated to either slope."}, {"text": "Any value of k works", "feedback": "Correct. Perpendicularity depends only on the slopes: 2 × (−½) = −1 regardless of k, so every value of k keeps these two lines perpendicular."}]'::jsonb,
 'perpendicularity-independent-of-intercept'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 43, 'Advanced',
 'Line L1 has equation 2x − 3y = 6. Line L2 is perpendicular to L1 and passes through the point where L1 crosses the x-axis. What is L2''s equation?', 1,
 '[{"text": "y = −³⁄₂x + 4½, through (0, 3)", "feedback": "L1''s x-intercept is where y = 0, not where x = 0 — recompute where L1 actually crosses the x-axis first."}, {"text": "y = −³⁄₂x + 4½", "feedback": "Correct. L1 rewrites to y = ⅔x − 2, crossing the x-axis at (3, 0); the perpendicular slope is −3/2, and 0 = −3/2(3) + b gives b = 4.5."}, {"text": "y = ⅔x − 2, shifted to pass through (3, 0)", "feedback": "This keeps L1''s own slope, which would make L2 parallel to L1 rather than perpendicular to it."}, {"text": "y = −³⁄₂x − 4½", "feedback": "The sign of b is off — recheck 0 = −3/2(3) + b, which gives a positive value of b."}]'::jsonb,
 'perpendicular-from-x-intercept'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 44, 'Advanced',
 'Quadrilateral ABCD has A(0, 0), B(3, 4), C(0, 8), and D(-3, 4). Using both side lengths and diagonal lengths, what is the most precise name for this shape?', 2,
 '[{"text": "Square", "feedback": "All four sides are equal (a rhombus), but check the two diagonals, AC and BD — they come out to different lengths, which rules out a square."}, {"text": "Parallelogram (but not a rhombus)", "feedback": "All four sides actually come out equal here (each measuring 5), which is a stronger result than a general parallelogram guarantees."}, {"text": "Rhombus (but not a square)", "feedback": "Correct. Every side measures √(3²+4²) = 5, making this a rhombus, but the diagonals AC = 8 and BD = 6 are different lengths — a square needs equal diagonals, so this rhombus isn''t a square."}, {"text": "Rectangle", "feedback": "A rectangle needs right angles but not necessarily equal sides — here all four sides are actually equal, which points toward a rhombus instead."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadrilateral-classification-multi-step'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 45, 'Advanced',
 'Quadrilateral ABCD has A(0, 0), B(3, 2), C(6, 0), and D(3, -5). What is the most precise name for this shape?', 1,
 '[{"text": "Rhombus", "feedback": "Check all four side lengths — AB and BC match each other, and CD and DA match each other, but the two pairs aren''t equal to each other."}, {"text": "Kite", "feedback": "Correct. AB = BC = √13 (one pair of adjacent sides) and CD = DA = √34 (the other pair of adjacent sides), with the two pairs unequal to each other — exactly the definition of a kite."}, {"text": "Parallelogram", "feedback": "Check the slopes of opposite sides, like AB and DC — a parallelogram needs both pairs of opposite sides parallel, which isn''t the case here."}, {"text": "Trapezoid", "feedback": "Check the slopes of both pairs of opposite sides — neither pair here turns out to be parallel."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadrilateral-classification-multi-step'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 46, 'Advanced',
 'Quadrilateral ABCD has A(0, 0), B(5, 1), C(7, 6), and D(1, 4). What is the most precise name for this shape?', 3,
 '[{"text": "Parallelogram", "feedback": "Check the slopes of AB versus DC, and AD versus BC — neither pair of opposite sides turns out to share a slope."}, {"text": "Kite", "feedback": "A kite needs two distinct pairs of adjacent sides to be equal in length — check all four side lengths here; none of them match another."}, {"text": "Trapezoid", "feedback": "Check the slopes of both pairs of opposite sides carefully — neither pair here is actually parallel."}, {"text": "A general quadrilateral (none of the above)", "feedback": "Correct. All four side lengths are different, and neither pair of opposite sides is parallel — this shape doesn''t fit any of the special quadrilateral categories."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadrilateral-classification-multi-step'),
('MPM2D', 'analytic-geometry', 'geometric-properties-of-shapes', 47, 'Advanced',
 'Quadrilateral ABCD has A(0, 0), B(4, 3), C(-2, 11), and D(-6, 8). Using both slope and side length, what is the most precise name for this shape?', 1,
 '[{"text": "Square", "feedback": "The sides here alternate between two different lengths (5 and 10), which rules out a square — a square needs all four sides equal."}, {"text": "Rectangle (but not a square)", "feedback": "Correct. AB and CD both measure 5, BC and DA both measure 10 (opposite sides equal), and the direction vectors of AB (4,3) and BC (−6,8) are perpendicular (their dot product is 4×−6 + 3×8 = 0) — right angles with unequal adjacent sides make this a rectangle, not a square."}, {"text": "Rhombus", "feedback": "The four sides here aren''t all equal — they alternate between lengths of 5 and 10, which rules out a rhombus."}, {"text": "Parallelogram (but not a rectangle)", "feedback": "Check the angle between adjacent sides AB and BC — their direction vectors are perpendicular, which is a stronger result than a general parallelogram guarantees."}]'::jsonb,
 'quadrilateral-classification-multi-step'),
